    -->

    <introduction>
      <autoOutline />
    </introduction>

    <section address="InstallingViaNuGet">
      <title>Adding MailKit to your project via NuGet</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          Using the <application>.NET CLI</application>, enter the following command from within
          your project directory:
        </para>
        <para>
          <command>dotnet add package MailKit</command>
        </para>
        <para>
          Or, in <application>Visual Studio</application>'s
          <externalLink>
            <linkText>Package Manager Console</linkText>
            <linkUri>https://learn.microsoft.com/nuget/consume-packages/install-use-packages-powershell</linkUri>
            <linkTarget>_blank</linkTarget>
          </externalLink>, enter the following command:
        </para>
        <para>
          <command>Install-Package MailKit</command>
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="Cryptography">
      <title>Adding S/MIME, OpenPGP, DKIM and ARC support</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          As of MimeKit 5.0, MimeKit's S/MIME, PGP/MIME, DKIM, ARC and DMARC support lives in the
          separate <application>MimeKit.Cryptography</application> package. If your application
          needs any of these features, you'll also need to add a reference to it:
        </para>
        <para>
          <command>dotnet add package MimeKit.Cryptography</command>
        </para>
        <para>
          In order for the parser to create cryptographic MIME entities such as
          <codeEntityReference>T:MimeKit.Cryptography.MultipartSigned</codeEntityReference>,
          <codeEntityReference>T:MimeKit.Cryptography.MultipartEncrypted</codeEntityReference> and
          <codeEntityReference>T:MimeKit.Cryptography.ApplicationPkcs7Mime</codeEntityReference>
          (instead of a plain <codeEntityReference>T:MimeKit.Multipart</codeEntityReference> or
          <codeEntityReference>T:MimeKit.MimePart</codeEntityReference>), the cryptography module
          must be initialized before any messages are parsed. The best place to do this is during
          your application's startup:
        </para>
        <code language="c#">
          using MimeKit.Cryptography;

          CryptographyModule.Initialize ();
        </code>
        <alert class="note">
          <para>
            The cryptography module is also initialized automatically the first time the
            <codeEntityReference>T:MimeKit.Cryptography.CryptographyContext</codeEntityReference>
            class is used (for example, when calling
            <codeEntityReference autoUpgrade="true">Overload:MimeKit.Cryptography.CryptographyContext.Register</codeEntityReference>),
            and it is safe to call
            <codeEntityReference>M:MimeKit.Cryptography.CryptographyModule.Initialize</codeEntityReference>
            more than once.
          </para>
        </alert>
      </content>
    </section>

    <section address="BuildingFromSource">
      <title>Building MailKit from Source</title>
      <content>
        <para>
          First, you'll need to clone <application>MailKit</application> from the
          <externalLink>
            <linkText>GitHub</linkText>
            <linkUri>https://github.com/jstedfast</linkUri>
            <linkTarget>_blank</linkTarget>
          </externalLink> repository:
        </para>
        <para>
          <command>git clone https://github.com/jstedfast/MailKit.git</command>
        </para>
        <para>
          Then, since <application>MailKit</application> makes use of git submodules, you
          will need to initialize and update the submodules using the following command
          from within the <application>MailKit</application> directory:
        </para>
        <para>
          <command>git submodule update --init --recursive</command>
        </para>
        <para>
          In the top-level <application>MailKit</application> source directory, there
          are several solution files:
        </para>
        <list class="bullet">
          <listItem>
            <para>
              <application>MailKit.sln</application> includes projects for .NET Framework 4.6.2, 4.7 and 4.8,
              .NETStandard 2.0 and 2.1, .NET 8.0, .NET 10.0, and the unit tests.
            </para>
          </listItem>
          <listItem>
            <para>
              <application>MailKit.Documentation.sln</application> includes projects for generating
              the documentation that you are reading right now.
            </para>
          </listItem>
        </list>
        <para>
          Once you've opened the appropriate <application>MailKit</application> solution
          file in <application>Visual Studio</application> (or any other IDE that supports
          SDK-style projects), you can choose the <application>Debug</application> or <application>Release</application>
          build configuration and then build.
        </para>
      </content>
    </section>

